fseterr: fix detection of glibc FILE flag macros

glibc 2.28 dropped the public _IO_ftrylockfile macro, and this glibc
reports __GNU_LIBRARY__ == 6 (the "== 1" test targets ancient libc5), so
the glibc branch of fseterr.c was never selected and configure/make
failed with:

  error: #error "Please port gnulib fseterr.c to your platform! ..."

_IO_EOF_SEEN and _IO_ERR_SEEN are still provided by <stdio.h>, so also
test _IO_EOF_SEEN, exactly as current gnulib does.

Signed-off-by: jeep <jeep@local>

--- bison-3.0.4.orig/lib/fseterr.c	2015-01-05 00:43:50.000000000 +0800
+++ bison-3.0.4/lib/fseterr.c	2026-10-01 21:39:10.262426485 +0800
@@ -29,7 +29,10 @@
   /* Most systems provide FILE as a struct and the necessary bitmask in
      <stdio.h>, because they need it for implementing getc() and putc() as
      fast macros.  */
-#if defined _IO_ftrylockfile || __GNU_LIBRARY__ == 1 /* GNU libc, BeOS, Haiku, Linux libc5 */
+#if defined _IO_EOF_SEEN || defined _IO_ftrylockfile || __GNU_LIBRARY__ == 1
+  /* GNU libc, BeOS, Haiku, Linux libc5.
+     glibc 2.28 no longer exposes _IO_ftrylockfile; _IO_EOF_SEEN is still
+     provided by <stdio.h> and selects the same glibc code path.  */
   fp->_flags |= _IO_ERR_SEEN;
 #elif defined __sferror || defined __DragonFly__ /* FreeBSD, NetBSD, OpenBSD, DragonFly, Mac OS X, Cygwin */
   fp_->_flags |= __SERR;
